{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1473257-the-rural-princedom-nebraska-by-night","id":1473257,"title":"The Rural Princedom: Nebraska by Night","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-01-25T07:13:51-08:00","id":1473257,"title":"The Rural Princedom: Nebraska by Night","content":"Following up on discussions on another thread, there is great potential in less urban settings to run a Camarilla chronicle that is rife with intrigue, and especially with cabin fever-style horror. City settings are often too familiar and comfortable; the demands of the Masquerade mean that Kindred are mostly safe if they stick to populated areas, and being able to mingle in Elysium removes a lot of tension in terms of the terror of the unknown. Camarilla politics also tend to be very stereotypical in terms of clan interactions; a less urban setting is great to explore original and unfamiliar concepts.<br /> <br /> Consider a highly generalizable setting: Nebraska by Night. Nebraska has 2 million people, but thanks to its vast livestock sector, supports 40-50 permanent vampire residents. The longstanding Prince of Nebraska is a Gangrel elder on the Road of the Beast, and at least half the residents are his brood of descendants. He is a staunch supporter of the Camarilla, which provides a framework for recognizing his absolute rights on domain and the domination of his childer. He is temperamental, impulsive, and an overall violent hair-trigger asshole; but he is respected from a distance by the greater Camarilla, as the Gangrel warlord regularly loans out his descendants as troops. Some of the older members of his brood come from rough-and-tumble times and are nearly as bestial; younger descendants from the 1900s are terrified of him. However, despite being embraced essentially as cannon fodder, many of them realize that they have plentiful blood and respect where they are, and elsewhere they'd be only one step up from Caitiff; they're not exactly Beckett-material. The next strongest group of vampires is a group of Nosferatu also on the Road of the Beast; they keep tabs on the Prince's brood, who have no way of piercing their Obfuscate, and are therefore in a state of abject fear and paranoia. The only other rural vampires are a few itinerant Malkavians who liven up the Ferals' gatherings, but are otherwise irrelevant. There are no elders of sufficient strength to challenge the Prince, and few outside the state who would actually want this princedom, so there's little prospect of change.<br /> <br /> In the city of Omaha, there's Elysium and the few representatives of the other clans. A Ventrue ancilla is his clan's primogen; he came out to the state in the 1800s to make money off of the burgeoning mining and agriculture sectors, and his few childer are placed to represent Ventrue interests in companies like Berkshire Hathaway there. A handful of Brujah are coterie-members with these Ventrue; they came originally as bodyguards, but nowadays take care of the more mundane tasks of running the city and covering Masquerade breaches, since the Ventrue can't be bothered with this. Neither clan sticks its head out of the city, and support the Prince while being essentially left alone. There's a small Tremere chantry here as well; which serves as a calm study retreat for those of the clan that desire it. Those who want to learn languages or read books in peace are comfortable with this temporary backwater posting, since there are no ambitious apprentices or such looking to muck up their lives. They are also on call for sorcerous services in case the Prince requests it, but are otherwise left alone.<br /> <br /> To outsiders looking in, this is a boring and very stable domain. In reality, there are two critical problems. The first one is the fact that Sabbat packs travel with near-impunity through the state, to such an extent that it is clear they have informants and collaborators in the princedom. The ones who are responsible for this are the bestial Nosferatu, who are actually members of the Sabbat. Their behavior is essentially identical to that of the Prince and his brood, so there's no obvious signs to suspect them and root them out. A number of the Prince's brood have turned as well, with the Vaulderie breaking their blood bonds. The Tremere could sort out this problem easily, but the Prince has no intention of giving anybody the impression that his rule is less than ironclad. Living in isolation and without the typical supernatural powers of detection, the Gangrel have no idea if and when their fellows turn. The Sabbat Nosferatu genuinely respect the Prince as a proud latter-day Feral and have no intention of eliminating him; he's the sort who would change sects to survive, and the Nosferatu only aim to turn enough vampires to make the state Sabbat in fact but not in name.<br /> <br /> The other threat to the domain is posed by Ravnos who occasionally drop by to wreak havoc. They never reveal themselves or openly confront any Gangrel, but instead use Chimerstry to terrorize them and ramp up paranoia. Any member of the other clans could find himself stalked by werewolves or ghosts, or witness something like the Prince brutalizing and diablerizing a descendant, or be confronted by Freak Legion-style fomori-looking monsters and have to fight for their lives (Horrid Reality). They have no way of suspecting the Ravnos of being behind all this, and neither do the players, since they don't have high enough Auspex and the illusions are staged with a great deal of thought. The Tremere in fact have an alliance with the Ravnos and tip them off about the locations of the havens of various vampires, and such. This is handled at a higher level than the state's residents: the Tremere chantry reports to the overseeing Lord, who passes on the information to Ravnos ancillae in Chicago and the West Coast, whose subordinates do not know the information comes from the Tremere. The Tremere do this because they are a small drop in the prairie; with an absolute minimum of exertion, they destabilize the domain enough to be unbothered in their pursuits by aggressive Feral assholes. The Ravnos, apart from the obvious entertainment value of pestering Gangrel without risk, specifically enjoy the opportunity of using Chimerstry to its full potential, with no regard to the Masquerade that they would have to show in a big city.<br /> <br /> This should be a lot of plot hooks and ideas for an atypical, seldom-explored Vampire setting!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13688,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13688-sultanabdal","user":"Sultanabdal","date":". 01-25-2022, 10:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13688-sultanabdal","name":"Sultanabdal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13688_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-26T01:55:52-08:00","id":1473325,"title":"","content":"Awesome idea! Nomad and rural games present very different challenges, compared to the classic VtM Elysium game, and you didi a very good joob of outlining a realistic and interesting scenario.<br /> <br /> I think &quot;Nomads&quot; for Requiem gave the best treatment to the subject of sparse and travelling coteries and groups of Vampires. I enjoiyed also that small bit in &quot;Alien Hunger&quot; detailing how roaming undeads could feed on cows on pastures near Denver, it made the game world feel very alive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}